# X-ray Diffraction Analysis of Crystalline Materials and Thin Films

## Overview
This experiment investigated crystalline materials using X-ray diffraction (XRD), including polycrystalline ZnO and copper samples, epitaxial ZnO thin films grown on sapphire, and an unknown oxide powder.

## Focus
- X-ray diffraction and Bragg's law
- Crystal structure identification and Miller indices
- Lattice-constant determination using Nelson–Riley extrapolation
- Epitaxial ZnO thin-film characterization
- Rocking-curve measurements and crystalline mosaicity
- Diffraction peak fitting and phase identification

## Key Results
- Polycrystalline ZnO lattice parameters:
  - a = (3.2480 ± 0.0008) Å
  - c = (5.2022 ± 0.0010) Å
- Copper lattice constant: (3.6093 ± 0.0029) Å
- Epitaxial ZnO thin-film lattice parameters:
  - a = (3.2492 ± 0.0002) Å
  - c = (5.2037 ± 0.0001) Å
- Unknown oxide identified as La₆Mo₂O₁₄

## Files
- `report.pdf` — Full experimental report
- `data/` — XRD measurements and analysis data

## Summary
The experiment applied X-ray diffraction to structural characterization of bulk crystalline materials and epitaxial thin films. Diffraction peak analysis, lattice-parameter extraction, and rocking-curve measurements were used to investigate crystallographic orientation, material identification, and structural quality.
